Article 3 Where the product manufactured by a patented method is not a new product, the plaintiff in a dispute over patent right infringement shall adduce evidence to prove the following facts:
(1) the product manufactured by the defendant and the product manufactured by using the patented method are identical products;
(2) it is more likely that the product manufactured by the defendant was produced by the patented method;
(3) the plaintiff has made reasonable efforts to prove that the defendant used the patented method.
After the plaintiff completes the adduction of evidence in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court may require the defendant to adduce evidence to prove that its product manufacturing method is different from the patented method.

Article 5 The plaintiff who brings an action for confirmation of non-infringement of intellectual property rights shall adduce evidence to prove the following facts:
(1) the defendant sent an infringement warning to the plaintiff or filed an infringement complaint against the plaintiff;
(2) the plaintiff sent a notice urging the exercise of the right of action to the defendant, and the time of such notice and its service;
(3) the defendant failed to initiate a lawsuit within a reasonable period.

Article 8 Where a party raises an objection to the following evidence formed outside the territory of the People's Republic of China solely on the ground that the evidence has not undergone formalities such as notarization or authentication, the People's Court shall not uphold such objection:
(1) evidence that has been confirmed by a judgment of a People's Court that has taken legal effect;
(2) evidence that has been confirmed by an effective award of an arbitration institution;
(3) evidence that can be obtained from official or public channels, such as publications and patent documents;
(4) evidence for which other evidence can prove its authenticity.

Article 11 The People's Court shall examine an application for evidence preservation by a party or an interested person taking into account the following factors:
(1) whether the applicant has provided preliminary evidence for its claim;
(2) whether the evidence can be collected by the applicant itself;
(3) the likelihood of the evidence being lost or becoming difficult to obtain in the future and its impact on proving the facts to be proved;
(4) the impact of the preservation measures that may be taken on the holder of the evidence.

Article 19 The People's Court may commission expert evaluation on the following specialized issues concerning the facts to be proved:
(1) the similarities and differences in means, functions, effects, etc. between the corresponding technical features of the accused infringing technical solution and the patented technical solution or the prior art;
(2) the similarities and differences between the accused infringing work and the work in which rights are claimed;
(3) the similarities and differences between the trade secret claimed by the party and information already known to the public in the relevant field, and between the accused infringing information and the trade secret;
(4) the similarities and differences in characteristics and traits between the accused infringing article and the authorized variety, and whether the differences are caused by non-genetic variation;
(5) the similarities and differences between the accused infringing integrated circuit layout design and the layout design for which protection is sought;
(6) whether the technology involved in the contract has defects;
(7) the authenticity and integrity of electronic data;
(8) other specialized issues requiring commissioned expert evaluation.

Article 23 The People's Court shall examine the expert opinion taking into account the following factors:
(1) whether the expert evaluator has the relevant qualifications;
(2) whether the expert evaluator has the knowledge, experience, and skills necessary to resolve the relevant specialized issues;
(3) whether the evaluation methods and procedures are standardized and whether the technical means are reliable;
(4) whether the submitted materials have been subject to party cross-examination and meet the conditions for evaluation;
(5) whether the basis for the expert opinion is sufficient;
(6) whether there are statutory grounds for the expert evaluator to withdraw;
(7) whether the expert evaluator engaged in any fraudulent or other conduct affecting impartial evaluation during the process.

Article 32 If a party claims that the amount of damages shall be determined by reference to a reasonable multiple of the intellectual property license fee, the People's Court may examine and determine the evidence of the license fee taking into account the following factors:
(1) whether the license fee was actually paid and the method of payment, and whether the license contract was actually performed or filed;
(2) the content, method, scope, and term of the licensed rights;
(3) whether there is a conflict of interest between the licensee and the licensor;
(4) the usual standards for licensing in the industry.
